"do","H935":"come","H1847":"knowledge","H7186":"severe","H859":"you(m.s.)","H1517":"sinew","H1270":"iron","H6203":"neck","H4696":"forehead","H5154":"bronze","H2962":"before","H6435":"lest","H559":"to say","H6090":"pain","H6459":"idol","H5262":"drink offering","H6680":"to command","H2372":"to see","H3605":"all","H2319":"New (Gate)","H6258":"now","H5341":"to watch","H3045":"to know","H1254":"to create","H6440":"before","H3117":"day","H2009":"behold","H1571":"also","H6605":"to open","H241":"ear","H898":"to act treacherously","H6586":"to transgress","H990":"abdomen","H4616":"because","H748":"to prolong","H639":"anger","H8416":"praise","H2413":"to restrain","H1115":"lest","H3772":"cut","H6884":"to refine","H3701":"money","H977":"to choose","H3564":"furnace","H6040":"affliction","H349":"how?","H2490":"to bore","H3519":"glory","H312":"another","H5414":"give","H413":"to(wards)","H589":"I","H1931":"he/she/it","H637":"also","H314":"last","H3027":"hand","H3245":"to found","H776":"country/planet","H3225":"right","H2946":"to extend","H8064":"heaven","H5975":"stand","H3162":"unitedness","H6908":"to gather","H4310":"who?","H853":"[Obj.]","H428":"these","H157":"lover","H2656":"pleasure","H894":"Babylon","H2220":"arm","H3778":"Chaldea","H1696":"speak","H6743":"to rush","H1870":"conduct","H7126":"come","H7218":"head","H5643":"secrecy","H6256":"time","H1961":"to be","H8033":"there","H136":"Lord","H3069":"YHWH/God","H7971":"depart","H7307":"spirit","H3541":"thus","H1350":"redeem","H6918":"holy","H3925":"teach","H3276":"to gain","H1869":"to tread","H3212":"to walk","H3863":"if","H7181":"to listen","H4687":"commandment","H5104":"River","H7965":"peace","H1530":"heap","H3220":"sea","H2344":"sand","H2233":"seed","H6631":"offspring","H4578":"belly","H4579":"grain","H8045":"to destroy","H1272":"to flee","H6963":"voice","H7440":"cry","H5704":"till","H7097":"end","H5650":"servant/slave","H6770":"to thirst","H2723":"desolation","H6697":"Rocks (of Goats)","H5140":"to flow","H1234":"to break up/open","H2100":"flowing","H369":"nothing","H7563":"wicked"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",1,null,"haydock","Waters; people, (Apocalypse xvii. 15.; Haydock) or from the stock of Juda, Proverbs v. 15. (Calmet) — He claimed the sovereign power, but had not the fortitude or wisdom of Israel. (Worthington)"],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","City: citizens of Jerusalem, Daniel iii. 28., and Matthew iv. 5."],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","Suddenly, when there was no human appearance of the event, as when I foretold the exploits of Cyrus and the ruin of Babylon so long before."],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Brass; unblushing, Jeremias iii. 3. (Calmet) — Os tuum ferreum. (Cicero, contra Pis.)"],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","Them. Could you have believed them? — Knowest not. He upbraids their ignorance and indocility."],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Knew. Therefore I did not speak of the liberation from Egypt, but from Babylon, which is represented as just taking place. (Calmet)"],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Opened; docile, (Theodoret) or acquainted with these things. Isaias first made known the captivity of Babylon, and its end; and he insists so much, that people may discern the truth of his predictions, and of religion. No atheist can, with a good conscience, hold out against his arguments, chap. xl., &c."],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","Bridle thee, like a headstrong beast, (Calmet) running to its own ruin. (Haydock) — God pardons freely, that people may be saved if they will. (Worthington)"],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Poverty, at Babylon. I have not treated thee with the utmost rigour, nor attempted to render thee free from every imperfection. (Calmet) — Septuagint, “Lo, I have sold thee, but not for silver; I have snatched thee from the,” &c. (Haydock)"],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Things, as I have done respecting Cyrus? (St. Cyril, &c.) who was a figure of Christ, ver. 15."],["haydock",16,null,"jerome","Spirit. The Fathers here find the three Persons of the blessed Trinity specified. Isaias was not from the beginning, though the text may also speak of him (Calmet) as he spoke long before the event, by divine inspiration. (Chaldean) (St. Jerome)"],["haydock",19,null,"haydock","Name. The Jews were not forgotten, till they had rejected the Messias."],["haydock",21,null,"jerome","Out. Their return was facilitated. This may easily be applied (Calmet) to the conversion of the Gentiles. (St. Jerome)"],["haydock",22,null,"haydock","Peace. Septuagint, “rejoicing,” or prosperity for the Chaldeans or wicked Jews, ver. 18. (Calmet) — It is promised only to the penitent. (Worthington)"]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"],"jerome":["Jerome",1,1,420,"https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/#jerome"]},"fa":[[9,null,0,"Conferences of John Cassian - Part I","5. Conference of Abbot Serapion",0,435,"… For it is better for a man to be troubled by the sin of vainglory than for him to fall into the desire for fornication, from which he either cannot recover at all or only with great difficulty after he has fallen. And this thought is admirably expressed byone of the prophets speaking in the person of God, and saying: \"For My name's sake I will remove My wrath afar off: and with My praise I will bridle thee lest thou shouldest perish,\" i.e., while you are enchained by the praises of vainglory, you cannot possibly rush on into the depths of hell, or plunge irrevocably into the commission of deadly sins."],[9,null,1,"Against Celsus","Book 5",1,253,"… This is confirmed by the prophet Isaiah.… Now the Scripture is appropriately adapted to the multitudes of those who are to peruse it, because it speaks obscurely of things that are sad and gloomy, in order to terrify those who cannot by any other means be saved from the flood of their sins, although even then the attentive reader will clearly discover the end that is to be accomplished by these sad and painful punishments on those who endure them. It is sufficient, however, for the present to quote the words of Isaiah: \"For my name's sake will I delay my anger, and my glory I will bring on you, that I may not destroy you.\""],[12,16,2,"The Proof of the Gospel","Book 5",2,339,"… So if we ask with reference to the words, \"He spake and they were made, he commanded and they were created,\" to which of the begotten beings He gave the command to create, we see now clearly that it was given to Him, Who said, \"My hand has laid the foundation of the earth, and my right hand has made the heaven strong\": Who also confesses that He was sent by One greater than Himself, when He says: \"Now the Lord, the Lord has sent me, and his Spirit. And it must be the Word of God Who said also, \"By the word of the Lord were the heavens made firm,\" if we compare the Psalm. …"],[13,null,3,"Against Hermogenes","AGAINST HERMOGENES 45",3,220,"He is the Lord's right hand, indeed his two hands, by which he worked and constructed the universe."],[16,null,4,"On The Trinity","Book 2",4,430,"… \"The Holy Ghost shall come upon thee, and the power of the Highest shall overshadow thee. And Matthew says, \"She was found with child of the Holy Ghost. Although, too, in the prophet Isaiah, Christ Himself is understood to say of His own future advent, \"And now the Lord God and His Spirit hath sent me.\""],[16,null,2,"The Proof of the Gospel","Book 6",2,339,"[Passage quoted, Isa. xlviii. 12 and 16.] You have here the Lord sent and the Lord sending, that is to say the Father and God of the Universe, entitled Lord twice as was usual."],[18,null,5,"On the Holy Spirit","Book 1",5,397,"… This well is clearly the grace of the Spirit, a stream proceeding from the living Fountain. The Holy Spirit, then, is also the fountain of eternal life.You observe, then, from his words that the unity of the divine greatness is pointed out and that Christ cannot be denied to be a fountain even by heretics, since the Spirit, too, is called a fountain. The Spirit is called a river, too, just as the Father said, \"Behold, I come down on you like a river of peace, and like a stream overflowing the glory of the Gentiles. And who can doubt that the Son of God is the river of life from whom the streams of eternal life flowed forth?"]],"fau":[["cassian","John Cassian",0,"father",null,0,435],["origen","Origen",0,"ecclesiastical-writer",null,1,253],["eusebius","Eusebius of Caesarea",0,"ecclesiastical-writer",null,1,339],["tertullian","Tertullian",0,"ecclesiastical-writer",null,0],["augustine","Augustine of Hippo",1,"father",null,1,430],["ambrose","Ambrose of Milan",1,"father",null,1,397]],"ftr":[["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 11 (1894)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf211"],["Ante-Nicene Fathers, vol. 4 (1885)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/anf04"],["W. J. Ferrar, The Proof of the Gospel (SPCK, 1920)"],["Ante-Nicene Fathers, vol. 3 (1885)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/anf03"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 1, vol. 3 (1887)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf103"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 10 (1896)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf210"]]}